WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.948
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.948 --> 00:00:04.383
[RUSTLING]

00:00:04.383 --> 00:00:08.766
[CLICKING]

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:16.900
PROFESSOR: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:20.050
We are starting a new
chapter on instrumentation.

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:24.250
And in this first section,
we'll discuss the interaction

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:26.090
of particles with matter.

00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:29.020
So what happens when
particles traverse

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:30.200
through a piece of material?

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:36.130
The underlying
principle of detection

00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:39.190
is that we do have to have
some sort of interaction

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:42.610
of the material of the detector
with the particles going

00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:43.450
through.

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:46.910
And there needs to be some
sort of transfer of energy

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:49.610
which can be identified.

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:52.630
Then, that piece
of energy can be

00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:57.460
amplified, separated
from noise, and so on.

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:00.580
But this first part of
any detection process

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:03.890
is this interaction of
this particle with matter.

00:01:03.890 --> 00:01:05.620
We can ask, what
kind of particles

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:08.770
can we actually identify?

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:12.550
Electrons, muons, pions, kaons,
protons, neutrons, heavy ions,

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:13.810
and photons.

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:16.000
But the key here in
this list of particles

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.620
is that those particles
have to be stable.

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:21.490
So we cannot directly
identify tau,

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:25.840
as the tau decays before it
has a chance to interact with

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:26.530
the detector.

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:30.550
The same for our top quark,
the Higgs boson, and so on.

00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:32.560
Interesting, neutrinos.

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:37.600
Neutrinos interact with the
detector very, very rarely.

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:40.330
When they do, they
actually detect a signal

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:43.540
that is not of the neutrino
directly, but of the products

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:47.070
of the interaction.

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:48.650
So we will split
this discussion up

00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:51.290
in the interaction
of neutral particles

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:54.660
and charged particles, and
we start with the photon.

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:57.050
So the photon interacts
with detector material.

00:01:57.050 --> 00:02:00.420
With material in general, we
have three leading effects.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:02.480
The photo effect,
Compton scattering,

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:06.080
and pair production.

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:07.700
In the photo effect,
we have a photon

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:12.380
interacting with an atom, and
then kicking out an electron.

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:14.450
And then, your
detector has a chance

00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:19.280
to identify the energy and
the momentum of the electron.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:22.260
This concept is used in
photo multiplier tubes

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:26.990
where then the kicked out
electron is further amplified.

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:29.630
And that leads to a
shower of electrons

00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:32.600
which can be measured like
the photodiode [? species ?]

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:34.280
effect.

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:37.400
We have discussed the
Compton effect quite a bit.

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:40.880
Here, the energy of
the scattered electron

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:44.360
can be measured out of the
energy of the scattered photon.

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:45.770
And then there's
pair production.

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:49.430
Pair production
dominated high energies.

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:52.040
And typically, it's
part of an initiation

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:55.730
process of electromagnetic
showers in calorimeters.

00:02:55.730 --> 00:02:56.420
That's great.

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:58.160
So what happens in
the calorimeter--

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:00.950
and we'll talk about
this more later--

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:05.930
is that an incoming photon or
electron causes this photon

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:09.170
to convert into pairs of
electrons and positrons.

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:12.590
And then there's this cascade
of electrons and positrons,

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:14.960
and additional photons
being produced.

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:17.490
In tracking detectors,
this is unwanted.

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:20.850
So therefore, build tracking
detectors rather thin.

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:22.970
We don't want to
have this confusion

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:24.920
of additional charged
particles, and we

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:29.330
try to measure the
energy of a proton.

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:33.340
So this plot here shows you
the cross-section as a function

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:34.940
of the photon energy.

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:38.080
And you see here very
nicely those three effects

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:40.060
contributing to the
total cross-section.

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:43.240
So for low energies--

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:48.500
in the range of some 100 keV,
the photo effect dominates.

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:52.930
And there is this intermediate
range from about 100 keV

00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:57.700
to about 10 MeV where we see the
effect of Compton scattering.

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:01.180
And everything above this is
dominated by pair production.

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:02.800
And this here shows
you that there

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:07.790
are some differences in what
kind of material you interact,

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:08.290
of course.

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:14.110
Photon or electron interaction.

00:04:14.110 --> 00:04:16.600
Again, the main
energy loss mechanism

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:19.149
for high energy photons
and electrons in matter

00:04:19.149 --> 00:04:21.970
is through pair production,
and also bremsstrahlung.

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:25.450
Bremsstrahlung is the effect
when an electron or positron

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:28.800
radiates a photon.

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:30.300
You can characterize
the materials

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:33.540
by introducing a concept
of radiation length.

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:35.952
And there's some
confusion sometimes.

00:04:35.952 --> 00:04:37.660
In the definition,
they are very similar,

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:39.370
but they're not quite the same.

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:43.050
Radiation length can be defined
as the length after which

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:46.230
an electron loses about
1 over e of its energy

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:48.000
by bremsstrahlung.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:50.230
And you often find
the definition

00:04:50.230 --> 00:04:52.860
through the mean
free path lengths.

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:54.990
And in X0, the
radiation length is

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:59.490
defined as 7/9 of the mean free
path length for pair production

00:04:59.490 --> 00:05:01.090
by a photon.

00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:03.840
So those are the
two definitions.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:05.760
And they're typically
used in the regime

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:09.690
where the process is dominant.

00:05:09.690 --> 00:05:13.140
It's a very convenient
property because of quantity,

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:18.060
because you don't have to worry
about when you're thinking

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:19.730
about the interaction
of the detector,

00:05:19.730 --> 00:05:22.230
about the specific thickness
and what it means in terms

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:26.040
of energy loss, and simply
know that your piece of lead

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:27.930
is a fraction of a
radiation length.

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:33.000
And that tells you how
many of your photons

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:37.820
or how much of the photon
energy is being lost.

00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:41.300
Typically, when you
build detector concepts

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:44.450
like a collider experiment
like ATLAS or CMS,

00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:48.500
you want the tracking volume
to be of low radiation length.

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:50.690
And for ATLAS and
CMS, this depends

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:53.570
on the rapidity or
the forward direction,

00:05:53.570 --> 00:05:58.430
but it varies between 30% and
200% of the radiation length.

00:05:58.430 --> 00:06:01.430
And for calorimeters, you
want that all the energy is

00:06:01.430 --> 00:06:03.010
deposited in the calorimeter.

00:06:03.010 --> 00:06:04.880
Nothing has leaked
out in the back,

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:07.730
and therefore, you design
calorimeters typically

00:06:07.730 --> 00:06:11.090
with 20 or 30 radiation
lengths [INAUDIBLE]..

00:06:13.790 --> 00:06:20.020
So again, when me think about
how a photon or an electron

00:06:20.020 --> 00:06:22.720
leaves a footprint
in a calorimeter,

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:24.790
you start from this first
electron and photon.

00:06:24.790 --> 00:06:29.830
And then, this particle evolves
in an electromagnetic shower.

00:06:29.830 --> 00:06:34.510
So there's this cascade
effect as the particle tries

00:06:34.510 --> 00:06:37.760
to move through this material.

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:40.130
The shower maximum
is given here.

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:41.700
Slightly depends on the energy.

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:43.880
It uses logarithmic dependency.

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:47.090
I introduce here the
critical energies.

00:06:47.090 --> 00:06:51.020
This is where the energy
loss through ionization

00:06:51.020 --> 00:06:53.225
is equal to the bremsstrahlung.

00:06:53.225 --> 00:06:54.725
And you see this
in this plot here--

00:06:54.725 --> 00:06:56.240
It's rather small--
as a function

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:57.540
of energy and the energy loss.

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:01.790
Again, you see, this effect
here is from ionization.

00:07:01.790 --> 00:07:03.950
And this effect here
is from bremsstrahlung.

00:07:03.950 --> 00:07:07.280
The critical energy is defined
as where those two energy loss

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:10.620
mechanisms give you
the same result.

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:12.260
So this is just a
normalization factor.

00:07:12.260 --> 00:07:15.740
But you see that there is
this logarithmic dependency

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:18.230
of the energy loss.

00:07:18.230 --> 00:07:21.830
You can also wonder how wide
a shower actually becomes.

00:07:21.830 --> 00:07:25.280
And this is given by the width.

00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:27.590
The transverse
width of the shower

00:07:27.590 --> 00:07:30.110
is given by the Moliere radius.

00:07:30.110 --> 00:07:33.110
And that's approximate.

00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:35.600
You find 21 MeV over
the critical energy

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:37.100
times the radiation
length gives you

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:42.530
the size of the transverse
sides of your shower.

00:07:42.530 --> 00:07:44.690
And in this example,
this is 8 centimeters,

00:07:44.690 --> 00:07:48.200
compared to a shower
length of 46 centimeters.

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:55.790
This is a very quick summary
of electromagnetic showers.

00:07:55.790 --> 00:07:58.130
You can also have nuclear
showers, of course.

00:07:58.130 --> 00:08:01.670
You have a neutron or a proton
entering your calorimeter.

00:08:01.670 --> 00:08:06.740
Here, the physics is a
little bit more complicated,

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:08.540
but you can introduce
similar concepts.

00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:11.930
This concept of radiation
lengths for strong interactions

00:08:11.930 --> 00:08:13.505
of the hadron with the nuclei.

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:19.250
So as for the
electromagnetic shower,

00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:20.870
there is this
cascade developing.

00:08:20.870 --> 00:08:23.375
However, if in the
cascade, for example,

00:08:23.375 --> 00:08:24.500
you would choose a neutron.

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:26.960
That neutron can travel
without leaving an interaction

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:28.290
for quite a distance.

00:08:28.290 --> 00:08:30.440
So you don't have
this continuous kind

00:08:30.440 --> 00:08:34.400
of flow of energy, and you have
little clusters of energies.

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:37.429
And in those clusters, you have
not just nuclear interaction,

00:08:37.429 --> 00:08:39.530
but you can also
produce new pions.

00:08:39.530 --> 00:08:42.679
And those new pions decay
into a pair of photons.

00:08:42.679 --> 00:08:45.660
And then, the photons, they
leave electromagnetic showers.

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:48.710
So hadronic showers have
typically two components--

00:08:48.710 --> 00:08:55.160
a hadronic part, which is
charged hadrons, pions, kaons,

00:08:55.160 --> 00:09:01.830
protons, neutrons, and an
electromagnetic part which

00:09:01.830 --> 00:09:06.500
is [INAUDIBLE] coming from
the decay of the neutrons.

00:09:06.500 --> 00:09:10.990
From the decay of the neutral
pions, which are photons.

00:09:10.990 --> 00:09:15.010
So here, just to give you a
feel four orders of magnitude,

00:09:15.010 --> 00:09:17.440
radiation length given--

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:20.150
the nuclear and the
electromagnetic radiation

00:09:20.150 --> 00:09:25.700
is given as a function
of Z. And for a gas,

00:09:25.700 --> 00:09:27.850
we're talking about
hundreds of meters.

00:09:27.850 --> 00:09:30.400
For light material--
aluminum and silicon--

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:32.080
we talk about 10 centimeters.

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:33.740
And for heavy
material-- specifically

00:09:33.740 --> 00:09:36.590
lead-- we're talking about
sub-centimeter radiation

00:09:36.590 --> 00:09:37.090
length.

00:09:40.127 --> 00:09:42.210
Moving from the neutral
particles from the photons

00:09:42.210 --> 00:09:44.020
and electrons--

00:09:44.020 --> 00:09:49.990
sorry-- to the charged
particle interactions.

00:09:49.990 --> 00:09:53.800
Here again, just summarizing
or giving a summary first,

00:09:53.800 --> 00:09:56.230
and then going through
the individual components.

00:09:56.230 --> 00:09:59.500
The interaction mechanisms
are multiple scattering--

00:09:59.500 --> 00:10:01.690
elastic scattering
with the atoms.

00:10:01.690 --> 00:10:03.370
This is a process
which is not very

00:10:03.370 --> 00:10:04.840
much wanted because,
when you try

00:10:04.840 --> 00:10:07.630
to monitor the trajectory
of the particle,

00:10:07.630 --> 00:10:09.940
you don't want it to
scatter and change randomly

00:10:09.940 --> 00:10:11.950
its direction or momentum.

00:10:11.950 --> 00:10:17.410
Ionization is a basic mechanism
for tracking detectors.

00:10:17.410 --> 00:10:19.630
Photon radiation is
an important part

00:10:19.630 --> 00:10:23.470
through bremsstrahlung but
also through Cerenkov radiation

00:10:23.470 --> 00:10:25.420
or transition radiation.

00:10:25.420 --> 00:10:28.390
And then, in scintillators,
you can excite the material.

00:10:28.390 --> 00:10:32.560
And then, if you have a
wavelength shifting fiber

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:36.280
material, you can cause
scintillation light

00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:37.760
to be shifted in wavelength.

00:10:37.760 --> 00:10:39.970
And then you can read
this out in order

00:10:39.970 --> 00:10:44.930
to gain information about
particles going through.

00:10:44.930 --> 00:10:45.430
All right.

00:10:45.430 --> 00:10:46.972
Let's start with
multiple scattering.

00:10:46.972 --> 00:10:49.780
So after passing a layer
of thickness with L,

00:10:49.780 --> 00:10:52.750
a particle with
some displacement r

00:10:52.750 --> 00:10:55.790
and some angle of deflection.

00:10:55.790 --> 00:10:59.710
So that is problematic
because you lose information

00:10:59.710 --> 00:11:01.370
through the random process.

00:11:01.370 --> 00:11:06.910
You see here this random
Gaussian-like distribution

00:11:06.910 --> 00:11:08.720
which is rather annoying.

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:11.770
So the key here is to
minimize the radiation lengths

00:11:11.770 --> 00:11:13.060
of the particle going through.

00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:21.440
The next part is an ionization.

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:25.020
Again, this is a primary
source of information

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:28.680
we gained from in
tracking detectors.

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:31.860
Typically, you have a number
of primary interactions

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:34.645
per unit length which
are Poisson distribution.

00:11:34.645 --> 00:11:36.270
So it's a random
process whether or not

00:11:36.270 --> 00:11:42.720
the particle sees an
atom which it can ionize.

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:45.630
And typically, in a
gas, you find about 30

00:11:45.630 --> 00:11:49.020
of those primary
interactions per centimeter.

00:11:49.020 --> 00:11:53.435
You have more in
denser materials.

00:11:56.846 --> 00:12:00.130
If you have kicked out an
electron in your ionization

00:12:00.130 --> 00:12:02.920
process, that electron
itself can again

00:12:02.920 --> 00:12:05.500
lead to secondary ionization.

00:12:05.500 --> 00:12:09.280
And once those electrons
reach sufficient energy,

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:12.250
they're sometimes visible as
individual tracks themselves.

00:12:12.250 --> 00:12:14.540
They called delta electrons--

00:12:14.540 --> 00:12:19.600
new particles which are visible
in your tracking detector.

00:12:19.600 --> 00:12:22.270
Energy fluctuations can
be really, really large

00:12:22.270 --> 00:12:23.290
through ionization.

00:12:23.290 --> 00:12:25.390
Sometimes, you have a
really tough interaction

00:12:25.390 --> 00:12:28.780
and you transfer a lot of
energy to the electron,

00:12:28.780 --> 00:12:33.420
while the mean number
is well under control.

00:12:33.420 --> 00:12:34.650
So, interesting.

00:12:34.650 --> 00:12:36.420
Just to give you
a feel, again, you

00:12:36.420 --> 00:12:38.820
have about 30
primary interactions

00:12:38.820 --> 00:12:40.680
per centimeters in gas.

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:43.860
The total ionization
energy you find

00:12:43.860 --> 00:12:47.850
is typically 3 times the
primary ionization energy.

00:12:47.850 --> 00:12:51.840
So you cause those seeds of
ionization, and then the energy

00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:57.040
to move away from
this initial track.

00:12:57.040 --> 00:13:00.490
When you look at the
energy loss distribution,

00:13:00.490 --> 00:13:03.070
this is a nice plot here
I made many, many years

00:13:03.070 --> 00:13:05.650
ago of the energy loss
in a piece of silicon

00:13:05.650 --> 00:13:07.000
of 100 gb pion.

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:12.130
So this pion loses its energy
primarily through ionization.

00:13:12.130 --> 00:13:20.310
And this is a small piece of
silicon which we used here.

00:13:20.310 --> 00:13:22.650
So you see this
typical distribution.

00:13:22.650 --> 00:13:24.780
It's called the
Landau distribution

00:13:24.780 --> 00:13:29.260
with a most probable value
and then a very long tail.

00:13:29.260 --> 00:13:31.890
And this tail here is dominated
by those delta electrons

00:13:31.890 --> 00:13:34.230
I was talking about.

00:13:34.230 --> 00:13:38.220
In bubble chamber or
cloud chamber pictures,

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:39.840
you see those delta
electrons here

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:44.910
as little curls of ionization
along the main part

00:13:44.910 --> 00:13:48.260
of your particle leading
an ionization track.

00:13:51.620 --> 00:13:56.390
The energy loss of
charged particles

00:13:56.390 --> 00:13:59.960
can be calculated using
the Bethe-Bloch formula.

00:13:59.960 --> 00:14:03.290
And it's a very good
description in a specific energy

00:14:03.290 --> 00:14:05.160
range-- in the
energy range which

00:14:05.160 --> 00:14:08.390
is dominated by ionization.

00:14:08.390 --> 00:14:10.370
And so, the formula
is given here.

00:14:10.370 --> 00:14:14.720
We discuss this some more
in our recitation section.

00:14:14.720 --> 00:14:17.750
But you see here in this
medium energy range,

00:14:17.750 --> 00:14:21.020
you are dominated with
Bethe-Bloch formula

00:14:21.020 --> 00:14:25.010
or by ionization where, when you
go into higher energies here,

00:14:25.010 --> 00:14:28.400
you find additional
energy loss--

00:14:28.400 --> 00:14:30.318
energy loss with radiation.

00:14:33.460 --> 00:14:38.500
So we can study the details
of this Bethe-Bloch formula.

00:14:38.500 --> 00:14:43.420
One interesting point is
the particle dependency

00:14:43.420 --> 00:14:47.320
of the energy loss-- and you
see this here shown for a muon,

00:14:47.320 --> 00:14:50.140
for a pion, and for proton.

00:14:50.140 --> 00:14:54.450
If you measure the energy
loss of a specific particle

00:14:54.450 --> 00:14:56.640
in a reasonable
momentum range, you

00:14:56.640 --> 00:14:58.290
can use that
information in order

00:14:58.290 --> 00:15:03.030
to learn which particle
travels through your detector.

00:15:03.030 --> 00:15:12.040
So you can use energy loss
in some cases in combination

00:15:12.040 --> 00:15:17.170
with the momentum measurement
in order to identify particles.

00:15:17.170 --> 00:15:20.300
Last but not least,
more radiation effects.

00:15:20.300 --> 00:15:22.420
Cerenkov radiation is
a very neat feature

00:15:22.420 --> 00:15:25.142
to also measure
particles as they

00:15:25.142 --> 00:15:27.850
go through a specific material.

00:15:27.850 --> 00:15:31.580
They can also be used in order
to identify particles again.

00:15:31.580 --> 00:15:35.170
So the idea here is
that Cerenkov radiation

00:15:35.170 --> 00:15:37.480
is emitted when
a particle passes

00:15:37.480 --> 00:15:41.290
through a dielectric medium with
a speed larger than the speed

00:15:41.290 --> 00:15:42.700
of the light in that medium.

00:15:42.700 --> 00:15:45.610
And that causes
a radiation cone.

00:15:45.610 --> 00:15:50.410
It's like a sonic boom when
you have airplanes passing by.

00:15:50.410 --> 00:15:54.010
And the simple picture is one
of the classical pictures.

00:15:54.010 --> 00:15:59.810
It's one of this wave front
cone under a specific Cerenkov

00:15:59.810 --> 00:16:00.310
angle.

00:16:03.020 --> 00:16:07.080
And then, last but not
least, transition radiation.

00:16:07.080 --> 00:16:10.445
This is a process which
were predicted by Ginzburg

00:16:10.445 --> 00:16:13.670
and Frenck in the 1940s.

00:16:13.670 --> 00:16:17.090
His idea is that a
photon is emitted

00:16:17.090 --> 00:16:20.660
when a charged particle
transfers through the boundary

00:16:20.660 --> 00:16:21.780
of two mediums.

00:16:21.780 --> 00:16:23.780
And so, if you
have a medium here

00:16:23.780 --> 00:16:29.590
in a vacuum, for example, if the
particle travels through here,

00:16:29.590 --> 00:16:32.980
it polarizes the
medium when it exits.

00:16:32.980 --> 00:16:37.060
And that polarization then
leads to an electric dipole

00:16:37.060 --> 00:16:38.560
which then starts to radiate.

00:16:38.560 --> 00:16:42.590
And you get a photon from
this type of radiation.

00:16:42.590 --> 00:16:44.980
So if you measure this
type of radiation,

00:16:44.980 --> 00:16:47.680
you might be able to identify
that the particle traveling

00:16:47.680 --> 00:16:50.395
through the transition of two
materials was an electron.

00:16:54.170 --> 00:16:55.150
All right.

00:16:55.150 --> 00:16:57.660
So this is the first
introduction to the topic.

00:16:57.660 --> 00:17:00.110
So in the next part, we
now have to understand

00:17:00.110 --> 00:17:04.270
how we use those phenomena
in order to build detectors.

